@mkbhd - The real challenge for self-driving vehicles isn’t navigating the clearly marked streets of Austin, TX, or San Francisco, CA — it’s surviving Indian road conditions in India. That’s the true test.

On Indian roads, you’ll encounter every mode of transportation imaginable: cars, two-wheelers, cyclists, tuk-tuks coming from all directions, vehicles entering a one-way from the wrong side just to save three minutes, lumbering trucks, nimble mini-trucks, and people darting across without warning. Add to that roadside shops spilling onto the road, and animals — cows, goats, dogs, cats — crossing at their own pace.

In just ten seconds inside a driverless car here, you’ll witness more chaotic, unpredictable scenarios than an autonomous vehicle in the U.S. might see in a week. If AI can master this, then it’s truly ready for the world.

